Z||000000||GT Bastard||10-22-2001||02:21 PM||||Anybody have a good method of doing this? There wasn't much useful info in the archives... The dremel tool seems to make it prettiest, but it takes forever!I've heard of using sandpaper, but how does that look?  It doesn't need to be perfect, but I want it to look nice.<P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/icare.jpg">  <p>[This message has been edited by GT Bastard (edited 10-22-2001).]||||reg||4
Z||000001||Raydar||10-22-2001||02:33 PM||||If you have air tools, a die grinder and various polishing wheels and compounds ought to work. <BR>That's how I polished my wheels. <P>------------------<BR>Raydar - aka Steve<BR><FONT SIZE="1"><BR>Black 88 Formula.<BR>Red 88 Duke coupe. "The Project" <BR>88 Formula parts car. "The Donor"<BR></FONT>||||reg||1
Z||000002||West Coast Fiero||10-22-2001||03:03 PM||||First off, go down to Home Depot and pick up some JASCO paint remover ( red and gold 1 gallon can ) - this will take the paint off with no questions asked - just DO NOT get it on your skin as it will burn very very bad. <P> Once thats done hose it off then take the die grinder to the casting marks and seams to get rid of them, them call up some local polishing places and get some quotes on polishing it, because all in all, it will take nearly a week and a half for the do-it-yourselfer to get it done righ<P>------------------<BR>Technician, West Coast Fiero<BR>www.cwestco.com<BR>310-305-4111||||reg||1
